Below are examples showing the difference between shallow extractions (bad) and rich extractions (good) from the same source material. Each example shows the source in the **exact format used in the Search Results section above** — note how the citation ID at the end of each Bad/Good extraction is copied verbatim from the `**ID:**` field of its source, never constructed from the title or document type.

### Example 1 — All facts from a single short source

**Source:**
> ### Result: Winter Launch
>
> - **ID:** 156a8004-337f-46a8-a67c-868399e6dadc
> - **Author:** Sam Okafor
> - **URL:** No URL
> - **Date:** 2024-11-27
>
> # Goal: Winter Launch
> January: Successful Winter 2025 Launch
> Creator: Sam Okafor
> Target date: 2025-01-27
> Status: on track
> Progress: 0%
> Completed at: 2025-01-31 (4 days late)

**Bad** (one detail, drops creator, target, and the on-track/0%-progress contradiction):
"The Winter 2025 Launch was completed on January 31, 2025, 4 days late.[^content:156a8004-337f-46a8-a67c-868399e6dadc]"

**Good** (every fact in the source folded into one learning, with the visible contradiction surfaced):
"The Winter 2025 Launch was a formal company goal created by Sam Okafor, targeted for January 27, 2025, and completed four days late on January 31, 2025. The goal was tracked as 'on track' with 0% recorded progress throughout, indicating a compressed, all-or-nothing final push to delivery rather than steady incremental progress.[^content:156a8004-337f-46a8-a67c-868399e6dadc]"

### Example 2 — Preserving a verbatim quote and the strategic framing it implies

**Source:**
> ### Result: Activation
>
> - **ID:** 28b2e01c-ae7e-4990-86e9-847d52610f2c
> - **Author:** Priya Raman
> - **URL:** No URL
> - **Date:** 2026-02-26
>
> # Goal: Activation
> Customers are actively using goals to run their business
> Creator: Priya Raman
> Owner: Jordan Ellery
>
> ### Update from Jordan Ellery at 2026-02-26
> It's not going well with this goal. This was the main goal I set at the start of the year, to become the system of record for goals for at least one ICP customer, and then figure out how to repeat it. We've evolved our thinking and focused more on posts as a way to slay decision recording and collaboration in a single feature. Our goals feature is pretty nice, so I think it requires us to revive some historical conversations about goals. We had hoped it would be Cartogram, but they are ghosting, as are Ledgerly. We aren't pursuing Beacon, Kernelworks is still on hold, etc. I think ultimately we're going to have to find a new ICP customer to adopt goals.

**Bad** (paraphrases away Jordan's verbatim quote, drops the named failed prospects, no strategic frame):
"The product strategy evolved from focusing on goals adoption to focusing on posts as the main wedge after the original goals adoption goal failed.[^content:28b2e01c-ae7e-4990-86e9-847d52610f2c]"

**Good** (preserves Jordan's verbatim quote attributed by name, names the failed prospects, surfaces the strategic-pivot framing):
"Jordan Ellery disclosed in a February 2026 update on the 'Activation' goal that 'we've evolved our thinking and focused more on posts as a way to slay decision recording and collaboration in a single feature' — a pivot away from goals-spreadsheet replacement as the primary wedge. The original goal was to make at least one ICP customer the system of record for goals; Cartogram and Ledgerly ghosted, Beacon was dropped, and Kernelworks stalled, forcing the team to broaden the search for a new ICP goals-adoption customer.[^content:28b2e01c-ae7e-4990-86e9-847d52610f2c]"

### Example 3 — Merging related facts from the same source under a phase frame

**Source:**
> ### Result: Annual Organisational Review
>
> - **ID:** fe5e1d07-93cc-4f27-8d53-066cecff7264
> - **Author:** Dana Whitfield
> - **URL:** No URL
> - **Date:** 2026-04-02
>
> Dana confirmed the annual review covered "the org changes and the tooling spend" including the promotion of Priya and the wind-down of the legacy marketplace product... "we budgeted for four contractors and ended up using two" and "the December infra migration slipped into January." The team agreed a fresh 409A valuation would be needed before the next equity grant cycle... This directly enables the team to issue new equity to recent hires like Taylor and Robin.

**Bad** (single fact, generic framing):
"Priya Raman was promoted from VP of Customer Experience to Chief of Staff, representing a significant organizational personnel milestone during the company's transition.[^content:fe5e1d07-93cc-4f27-8d53-066cecff7264]"

**Good** (merges related facts from the same source, explains why it matters):
"Priya Raman was promoted from VP of Customer Experience to Chief of Staff during 2025, and the company hired Taylor Voss and Robin as new employees, necessitating a 409A valuation to enable equity grants. These personnel changes reflect a deliberate organizational build-out phase in 2025, consistent with a company scaling its team to support a new product direction after a strategic pivot.[^content:fe5e1d07-93cc-4f27-8d53-066cecff7264]"

### Example 4 — Named participants, verbatim quotes, and architectural reasoning

**Source:**
> ### Result: Mailbox refactoring meeting
>
> - **ID:** 0b807a8a-2ca6-446a-831d-1b3a1df509e7
> - **Author:** Marcus Iyer
> - **URL:** https://meet.example.com/xxx-xxxx-xxx
> - **Date:** 2026-03-24
>
> Elias explained that "mailbox is very, very tied to email" and they "shoehorned posts in" using workarounds... Devin sketched a design where "collaboration.mailbox" contains a mailbox class... Marcus confirmed: "lots of small simple REST APIs, not one giant mailbox entries API." The team decided to have both a shared mailbox_entry table AND separate email_mailbox_entry and chat_mailbox_entry tables... Marcus emphasized "it's a lot easier if you're just like get me the mailbox entries for this person."

**Bad** (states the decision but drops the people, quotes, and reasoning):
"Engineering is working on a major refactoring of the mailbox system to support chat messages alongside emails in the inbox.[^content:0b807a8a-2ca6-446a-831d-1b3a1df509e7]"

**Good** (names participants, includes direct quotes, explains the architectural reasoning):
"The engineering team undertook a major architectural refactoring of the mailbox system to support chat messages alongside emails, moving the mailbox model from the email domain into a shared 'collaboration layer' abstraction with a unified mailbox_entry table alongside separate email_mailbox_entry and chat_mailbox_entry tables. Principal engineer Marcus Iyer explicitly advocated to 'take the time and be like okay we have a whole cycle here... really think about it, put it through its paces,' prioritizing long-term architectural clarity over short-term velocity.[^content:0b807a8a-2ca6-446a-831d-1b3a1df509e7]"

